🚀 Day 13 of My DevOps Troubleshooting Journey | Jenkins

Today I worked on a common real-time Jenkins issue.

🔴 Scenario: Jenkins Pipeline Fails During Docker Build

The pipeline was working perfectly until yesterday, but suddenly the Docker build stage started failing.

Pipeline status:

✅ Git Clone
✅ Maven Build
✅ Unit Tests
❌ Docker Build

The error was:

```text
Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at unix:///var/run/docker.sock
Is the docker daemon running?
```

🔍 How would I troubleshoot it?

As a DevOps Engineer, I would follow a systematic approach:

1️⃣ Check whether the Docker service is running on the Jenkins agent.

2️⃣ Verify Docker installation and version.

3️⃣ Check whether the Jenkins user has permission to access Docker.

4️⃣ Check the permissions of `/var/run/docker.sock`.

5️⃣ Confirm that Jenkins is running the job on the expected agent/node.

6️⃣ Verify Jenkins agent connectivity.

7️⃣ Check Docker daemon and system logs for errors.

8️⃣ Review the Jenkins console output to identify exactly where the failure occurs.

9️⃣ Fix the actual root cause instead of immediately restarting Jenkins.

🔟 Re-run the pipeline and verify that the Docker image is built successfully.

💡 Key Learning

A Jenkins pipeline failure doesn't always mean Jenkins itself is the problem.

The issue could be with the Jenkins agent, Docker daemon, Docker socket permissions, or agent configuration.

The important thing is to troubleshoot layer by layer and identify the actual root cause before applying a fix.

DevOps is not just about building pipelines. It's also about knowing how to troubleshoot them when things break.💻
